          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When one code already covers both a condition and its manifestation, is it wrong to add a second code for the manifestation?",
          "answer": "**What the passages say**\n\n- In the ICD-10-CM code structure guidance, \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" work as a pair. They fix the order in which an underlying condition and its manifestation are reported. \"Code also\" means two codes are needed, without setting an order. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- Where a combination code exists, it is used instead of separate codes. (Same source.)\n- Even with a combination code, some conditions still need a second code for detail. The diabetes guidance gives two examples: a diabetic chronic kidney disease code is paired with a code for the stage, and a diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for site and depth. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\n**What this suggests**\n\nAdding a second code is not automatically wrong. Whether it is allowed depends on the specific instructions attached to the codes involved. Those instructions can require a second code, forbid one, or leave it optional.\n\n**What the passages did not cover**\n\nThe retrieved material does not explain the Excludes1 and Excludes2 notes in full, and it does not list the exact rules for each code family. For a specific case, check the instructional notes printed beside the codes in the official ICD-10-CM tabular list, or ask a trained medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
